WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:07.262
Let's start with what has happened at our own mines.

00:07.262 --> 00:10.425
MSHA's records for the old Clover Bottom mine show

00:10.425 --> 00:13.100
four times when rock fell from the face or the

00:13.100 --> 00:16.200
back onto miners, between two thousand five and

00:16.200 --> 00:18.688
two thousand fourteen. Every one of them happened

00:18.688 --> 00:21.525
during scaling, checking a room, or preparing a

00:21.525 --> 00:24.538
shot. In the last one, the rock above the piece

00:24.538 --> 00:26.488
being pulled down came down.

00:27.625 --> 00:30.187
That's the lesson of this video. The most dangerous

00:30.187 --> 00:32.137
moments are often the ones where you're trying

00:32.137 --> 00:33.737
to make the ground safe.

00:34.854 --> 00:37.054
At Boonesboro in two thousand twenty,

00:37.054 --> 00:39.429
a scaler lost a tooth off its pick.

00:39.429 --> 00:41.579
The operator walked toward the face to pick it

00:41.579 --> 00:44.754
up, and a rock fell from the face onto his shoulder.

00:44.754 --> 00:47.779
A small tool, and a rock that was not examined.

00:47.779 --> 00:50.429
Never walk up to a face to retrieve a tool.

00:50.429 --> 00:53.454
Report it, and let the area be examined and scaled

00:53.454 --> 00:54.404
first.

00:55.536 --> 00:57.974
At Dix River, production has been mostly on the

00:57.974 --> 01:01.236
surface, but headers are now being mined underground

01:01.236 --> 01:04.161
on two levels, in the same Camp Nelson Limestone

01:04.161 --> 01:06.648
worked at Boonesboro. More people are working under

01:06.648 --> 01:09.248
a back. So the habits in this video matter there

01:09.248 --> 01:09.948
too.

01:13.294 --> 01:16.520
Here's the rule in Part 57. Ground that creates

01:16.520 --> 01:19.832
a hazard to persons has to be taken down or supported

01:19.832 --> 01:22.557
before other work or travel is allowed in that

01:22.557 --> 01:24.982
area. Until the correction is done,

01:24.982 --> 01:27.495
the area has to be posted with a warning against

01:27.495 --> 01:29.882
entry. And if it's left unattended,

01:29.882 --> 01:32.745
a barrier has to be installed to keep people out.

01:33.868 --> 01:36.268
Notice the word "before." You don't work under

01:36.268 --> 01:37.931
it and fix it later.

01:39.058 --> 01:42.858
Part 57 also says who looks. The operator designates

01:42.858 --> 01:45.946
people who are experienced in examining and testing

01:45.946 --> 01:49.383
for loose ground. Supervisors or other designated

01:49.383 --> 01:52.096
persons examine, and where it applies test,

01:52.096 --> 01:54.058
the ground where work will be done.

01:54.058 --> 01:56.784
That's before work starts, after blasting,

01:56.784 --> 01:59.209
and as conditions call for during the shift.

02:00.328 --> 02:02.965
As a new miner, you're not that person yet.

02:02.965 --> 02:05.028
But you're still the extra pair of eyes on every

02:05.028 --> 02:05.928
shift.

02:09.242 --> 02:11.929
Before you go into a place, stop and look up and

02:11.929 --> 02:14.992
around. Look for cracks, loose slabs,

02:14.992 --> 02:18.392
spalling, water, and fresh rock on the floor,

02:18.392 --> 02:20.779
which tells you something has already come down.

02:20.779 --> 02:23.167
Look at the ribs and the pillars too.

02:23.167 --> 02:25.105
If something looks different from yesterday,

02:25.105 --> 02:26.317
that's information.

02:27.428 --> 02:30.303
Ground also talks. Ticking, cracking,

02:30.303 --> 02:33.041
or a rock dropping from above are warnings.

02:33.041 --> 02:35.778
If you hear them, don't wait to see what happens.

02:35.778 --> 02:37.953
Back out to ground you know is supported,

02:37.953 --> 02:40.691
keep other people out, and report it right away.

02:41.822 --> 02:44.185
A trained person can sound the ground by tapping

02:44.185 --> 02:47.147
it with a bar. Solid rock tends to ring.

02:47.147 --> 02:50.160
Loose rock sounds dull or hollow, sometimes called

02:50.160 --> 02:52.622
drummy. That's a skill that takes training and

02:52.622 --> 02:55.597
practice. A trainee doesn't do it alone.

02:56.720 --> 02:59.683
Scaling means taking down loose rock on purpose.

02:59.683 --> 03:02.858
Part 57 says it must be done from a location that

03:02.858 --> 03:05.945
doesn't expose anyone to falling material,

03:05.945 --> 03:08.095
or with other protection from it. And where it's

03:08.095 --> 03:10.970
done by hand, the bar has to be long enough,

03:10.970 --> 03:13.683
and designed, so you can remove loose rock without

03:13.683 --> 03:14.945
being exposed.

03:16.058 --> 03:18.370
At our sites, you scale only if you're trained

03:18.370 --> 03:20.970
and authorized. Otherwise you report it,

03:20.970 --> 03:23.745
and you stay clear. Even trained people plan their

03:23.745 --> 03:26.020
exit before they pull the first rock,

03:26.020 --> 03:28.483
because rock above can let go when rock below it

03:28.483 --> 03:29.383
moves.

03:30.500 --> 03:33.475
After scaling, the area is looked at again.

03:33.475 --> 03:36.375
If the ground still isn't right, it needs support.

03:36.375 --> 03:39.763
Part 57 requires ground support where conditions,

03:39.763 --> 03:43.287
or experience in similar ground, show it's necessary.

03:43.287 --> 03:45.450
Until the area has been re-examined,

03:45.450 --> 03:47.288
treat it as unsafe.

03:48.411 --> 03:52.136
Blasting loosens ground. That's why Part 57 lists

03:52.136 --> 03:54.673
"after blasting" among the times the ground gets

03:54.673 --> 03:58.123
examined. After a shot, and after the air is cleared

03:58.123 --> 04:00.935
as covered in Module 11, nobody goes under the

04:00.935 --> 04:03.698
new face until the designated person has looked

04:03.698 --> 04:06.511
at it. A face that looks fine from a distance can

04:06.511 --> 04:08.761
still have loose rock hanging in it.

04:09.872 --> 04:12.559
Say you're walking a travelway and you see a slab

04:12.559 --> 04:14.947
hanging out of the back, with fresh rock on the

04:14.947 --> 04:18.685
floor beneath it. Stop. Back out to supported ground.

04:18.685 --> 04:21.372
Tell anyone coming that the area isn't safe.

04:21.372 --> 04:24.085
Report it to your supervisor and describe exactly

04:24.085 --> 04:26.585
where it is. Don't try to knock it down yourself

04:26.585 --> 04:29.122
if you haven't been trained and authorized.

04:29.122 --> 04:31.947
A good report today is how the area gets posted,

04:31.947 --> 04:34.560
scaled, and checked before the next person walks

04:34.560 --> 04:35.347
under it.

04:36.458 --> 04:38.983
Surface highwalls need the same respect.

04:38.983 --> 04:42.346
Loose material on a highwall or a bank is examined

04:42.346 --> 04:44.883
and controlled, and you stay back from the toe

04:44.883 --> 04:47.033
unless you've been told it's been examined.

04:47.033 --> 04:50.133
Underground, your site's ground control plan applies,

04:50.133 --> 04:51.608
not highwall rules.

04:54.924 --> 04:57.324
Never go under ground that hasn't been checked,

04:57.324 --> 05:00.449
scaled, or supported. Never walk up to a face to

05:00.449 --> 05:03.524
pick up a tool or take a look. And a sign or a

05:03.524 --> 05:06.237
barrier means stop. If you cross one,

05:06.237 --> 05:08.512
you're standing where somebody decided the ground

05:08.512 --> 05:09.637
isn't safe.

05:10.758 --> 05:13.208
If you see something, tell your supervisor,

05:13.208 --> 05:15.495
and keep people away until it's dealt with.

05:15.495 --> 05:18.133
Ask where your site's ground control plan is kept,

05:18.133 --> 05:21.120
and ask to read it. It sets out how your mine controls

05:21.120 --> 05:23.370
its ground. Your lead can show you.

05:24.504 --> 05:27.904
To recap: look, listen, stay out from under,

05:27.904 --> 05:31.329
and report. Scale only if you're trained and authorized.

05:31.329 --> 05:33.879
And remember what the record says: at one of our

05:33.879 --> 05:36.879
mines, every face fall happened while someone was

05:36.879 --> 05:38.704
trying to make the ground safe.

05:39.834 --> 05:42.809
Your site's plans always override this video.
